<p>PerceptionLM (PLM) takes the open path: Perception Encoder as the vision backbone, Llama 3 as the language decoder, a data engine built entirely from open-source models, and 2.8M <em>human</em>-labeled video annotations for the gaps synthetic data cannot fill. The result: a model that beats GPT-4o on fine-grained video understanding — without distilling from GPT-4o.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 THE SYNTHETIC SCALING WALL -->
<section id="s-wall" data-code="wall">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Synthetic Scaling Wall</div>
<h2>Synthetic Data Hits a Wall at "Hard" Tasks</h2>
<p>The PLM team started by running a controlled synthetic data scaling study. They varied model size (1B / 3B / 8B), encoder size, and resolution across 20 benchmarks — and fit power laws to the compute-vs-error curves. The results confirmed a clean log-log relationship: more FLOPs → lower error, following <strong>Error = β × FLOPs<sup>α</sup></strong>.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Power Law Fit</div>
  Error = <span class="math-var">β</span> × GFLOPs<sup><span class="math-var">α</span></sup><br>
  <span class="math-comment">// α = scaling exponent. Smaller = better (steeper improvement per FLOP)</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment">// Standard Video QA:  α ≈ −0.15  (good scaling)</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment">// Hard Video Tasks:   α ≈ −0.03  (nearly flat — synthetic data fails here)</span>
</div>

<p>The alarming finding: for <strong>fine-grained video understanding</strong> — tasks requiring nuanced understanding of <em>how</em> an action is performed, <em>where</em> in the frame, or <em>how many times</em> — synthetic data barely helps at all. The exponent is −0.03, five times flatter than the −0.15 for standard video QA.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">synthetic data scaling</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to compare</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <button class="stc-btn active" id="wall-btn-both" onclick="wallShow('both')">Both</button>
      <button class="stc-btn" id="wall-btn-std" onclick="wallShow('std')">Standard Video QA (−0.15)</button>
      <button class="stc-btn" id="wall-btn-hard" onclick="wallShow('hard')">Hard Video Tasks (−0.03)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="wall-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem;text-align:center;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
      X: Synthetic compute (GFLOPs, log scale) · Y: Benchmark Error · each dot = one model config
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The implication is stark: you could throw 10× more synthetic data at the hard video tasks and barely move the needle. The knowledge needed to answer "In which direction is the person moving the sandpaper?" or "How many times does the person flip the chakli?" simply isn't present in synthetically generated captions — and more caption volume doesn't create it.</p>
<p>This motivates the core contribution: <strong>PLM-FGQA</strong> and <strong>PLM-STC</strong> — two large-scale human-annotated datasets targeting exactly these gaps.</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 PLM-FGQA -->
<section id="s-fgqa" data-code="fgqa">
<div class="section-no">03 — PLM-FGQA</div>
<h2>2.4 Million Fine-Grained Questions Humans Wrote</h2>
<p>PLM-FGQA is the largest human-annotated video QA dataset by a wide margin — <strong>8× larger</strong> than the previous biggest. But more important than scale is <em>coverage</em>: existing datasets were severely under-representing the fine-grained question types that hard video understanding actually requires.</p>

<p>The chart below shows each question type and how many times more examples PLM-FGQA has compared to all existing datasets combined. <strong>Click any row</strong> to see an example question-answer pair for that type.</p>

<div class="vp">
