<p>The data was collected through a multi-stage process: temporal clustering and shot detection first identifies video segments with salient actions; a data engine (PLM itself in an early version, plus text-only Llama 3) generates candidate questions and answers; then human annotators watch the video and verify or replace each answer. The result is 2.4M verified QA pairs across 780K unique video clips.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>780K</strong> unique video clips from cooking, DIY, carpentry, automotive repair, and more</li>
  <li><strong>9 fine-grained question types</strong> — from movement direction to counting to spatial relationships</li>
  <li>Spans both <strong>egocentric and third-person</strong> viewpoints</li>
</ul>
</section>

<!-- §04 PLM-STC -->
<section id="s-stc" data-code="stc">
<div class="section-no">04 — PLM-STC</div>
<h2>Teaching "Where" and "When": Spatio-Temporal Captions</h2>
<p>Even if a model can answer fine-grained questions about <em>what</em> is happening, it may have no idea <em>where</em> in the frame or <em>when</em> during the video. That's what PLM-STC addresses: 476K spatio-temporal captions, each tied to both a time interval <strong>and</strong> a masklet — a high-fps sequence of pixel-level segmentation masks tracking the subject through the video.</p>
<p>This is qualitatively different from prior datasets that use bounding boxes (coarse, axis-aligned) or just temporal intervals (no spatial info). A masklet precisely traces the subject even as it rotates, occludes, or exits frame.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">spatio-temporal caption viewer</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">walk through segments</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="stc-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;border-radius:8px"></canvas>
    <div class="stc-controls">
      <button class="stc-btn" id="stc-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button class="stc-btn" id="stc-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="stc-seg-label" id="stc-seg-label">Segment 1 of 4</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The STC dataset generates three training tasks from each annotation:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>RCap</strong>: Given region + timestamps → generate caption describing the event</li>
  <li><strong>RTLoc</strong>: Given region + caption → localize to time interval (inverse of RCap)</li>
  <li><strong>RDCap</strong>: Given region → generate all (start, end, caption) tuples for the full video</li>
</ul>
<p>Together these force the model to reason bidirectionally: from video → language <em>and</em> from language → video timestamps. Open-source baselines essentially fail at these tasks (scoring 1–6% on RDCap), while PLM-8B achieves 59.1% — making this dataset uniquely high-leverage.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 ARCHITECTURE & TRAINING -->
<section id="s-arch" data-code="arch">
<div class="section-no">05 — Architecture & Training</div>
<h2>Three Stages from Projector to Specialist</h2>
<p>PLM combines a <strong>Perception Encoder</strong> (PE-L/14 for 1B/3B, PE-G/14 for 8B) with a <strong>Llama 3</strong> language decoder, connected by a 2-layer MLP projector. Images use dynamic tiling up to 36 tiles at 448² with 2×2 pooling. Video uses 32 frames at 448² with the same spatial pooling.</p>
<p>Training proceeds through three carefully staged phases. Walk through them below:</p>

